# Git Conventions

## Commit Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <short description>

<body — detailed, required for agent commits>
```

### Types

| Type | When |
|---|---|
| `feat` | New game mechanic, system, or feature |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `refactor` | Code refactor without behavior change |
| `data` | ScriptableObject, config, balance changes |
| `art` | Sprites, animations, UI assets |
| `test` | Tests |
| `ci` | GitHub Actions, build scripts |
| `docs` | Documentation, README |
| `chore` | Package dependencies, project settings |

### Scopes (optional)

`combat`, `units`, `ui`, `inventory`, `guild`, `ai`, `audio`, `scene`, `feel`

### Rules

- Language: **English**
- Use imperative: "add", "fix", "remove" — not "added", "fixes"
- No period at the end of the subject
- Max 72 characters in the subject line — **no author markers in the title**

### Authorship (agent commits)

Commits are authored by **Max Gaida only**. Never add `Co-authored-by:` (or any other
authorship trailer) — Max asked for a clean, single-author history.

An agent still has to say it was an agent, so Max can tell agent commits from his own
and audit them separately. Put it in the **body**, not the subject:

- first line of the body: `Cursor Auto commit.` (or the agent's own name)
- then a body detailed enough that Max — or another agent in a later session — can audit
  the change without re-reading the diff:
  - what changed (files/systems, not every line)
  - why / intent
  - how to verify (Play steps, toggles, edge cases)
  - anything intentionally left out or risky

Rules the agent does **not** get to change on its own: this file, project conventions,
and anything under `.cursor/rules`. Propose it to Max instead.

Stage **only your own** changes: use explicit paths, never `git add -A`. Max and other
agents may have unfinished work in the tree — sweeping it into your commit steals
authorship and hides someone else's work-in-progress in an unrelated change.

**This rule is enforced mechanically, not by memory** (2026-07-30). A `PreToolUse` hook in
`.claude/settings.json` denies `git add -A|--all|-u|.` and `git commit -a|-am` outright.
The reason it needed a barrier: the rule is **one-sided**. Following it protects other
sessions from you, but nothing protects *your* uncommitted work from a session that sweeps
the tree — and that is exactly how a finished test ended up inside an unrelated commit that
same day. A directory in the pathspec (`git add -- Assets/_Project/Art`) passes the hook but is
**not** an exemption and not safe: someone else's uncommitted edits may sit inside it, and a
pathspec commit takes files **from disk**, ignoring the index — so `git restore --staged` cannot
pull them back out. List files, not folders, and read `git diff --cached --name-only` before
committing.

The hook only refuses the flags that mean "everything", and it covers Claude Code sessions in this
project only — a sweep typed in a terminal or clicked in an IDE still gets through.

Corollary worth keeping: **commit as soon as a step is done.** The window between finishing
work and committing it is exactly the window in which someone else's sweep can claim it.

### Examples

```
feat(combat): add poison damage over time mechanic

Cursor Auto commit.
Adds PeriodicDamage stacking on Ignition. Wired through DamagePipeline.
VERIFY: ignite dummy, confirm DoT ticks and refresh; no armor on True.
```

```
fix(units): fix archer not targeting nearest enemy
data(combat): rebalance warrior base stats
refactor(ai): extract targeting logic into separate class
chore: update Unity packages
```

